  "result": "The 500s came from prices with thousands separators, and they're fixed. `npm test` passes (7 of 7).\n\n**Root cause:** `parseAmount` in `src/money.js` only matched plain digits, like `7` or `12.50`. For a price such as `\"2,450.50\"` or `\"1,299.00\"`, the regex returned `null`. The next line then read `m[1]` on `null`, which threw `TypeError: Cannot read properties of null (reading '1')`. `server.js` caught that and returned a 500.\n\nIn `logs/app.log`, the debug line just before each of the first two 500s I checked has a comma-formatted price (lines 171 and 234). I did not check the payload for every 500 in the log.\n\n**Fix (`src/money.js`):**\n- `parseAmount` now accepts properly grouped thousands separators (`1,299.00`, `2,450.50`).\n- Input that isn't an amount now throws the documented `Error('Invalid amount: <input>')`. Before, it crashed with the `TypeError`.\n- Malformed input like `\"1,23\"` is still rejected, not silently accepted.\n\n**Behaviour to know about:** `checkout` still turns an invalid price into a 500, because `server.js` catches every error that way. A bad price is really a client error, so returning a 400 for it would be a sensible follow-up. I left it alone because you didn't ask for it.\n\n**Tests (`test/money.test.js`):** I added three tests. They cover comma-formatted amounts, a descriptive error for invalid input, and a `checkout` call with a `\"1,299.00\"` price. The first and third would have failed before the fix.",
